# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded — the standalone runtime does not activate native libraries shipped in its installation prefix
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: packaging-runtime
#+DEPENDS: dwg-write-rejects-a-drawing-built-in-memory
#+BRANCH: fix-clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

A complete clautolisp installation can contain the native DWG libraries and
still run as though no DWG codec were installed.

SCHME+ pipeline 86651 provides a concrete end-to-end reproduction.  Its Linux
build job 514668 built clautolisp 2.2.85 from =master=, then successfully built
and installed both native objects:

#+begin_example
[100%] Linking C shared library libredwg.so
built: .../clautolisp/drawing-dwg/source/clal_dwg.so
LibreDWG runtime: .../third-party/libredwg/build/libredwg.so
installed: .../libredwg.so -> .../.clautolisp/lib/clautolisp/linux/x86-64/
installed: .../clal_dwg.so -> .../.clautolisp/lib/clautolisp/linux/x86-64/
#+end_example

The resulting =.clautolisp/= prefix was passed intact as an artifact to the
test jobs.  Nevertheless job 516228, running that installed standalone
clautolisp, failed an ActiveX =Document.SaveAs= to DWG with:

#+begin_example
SaveAs could not write .../schme-test-a1.dwg:
no writer codec registered for format :DWG
#+end_example

This is not a missing compiler and not a failed native build.  The distributed
libraries exist at runtime but clautolisp does not activate them.

The Windows release has the same architectural risk with =libredwg.dll= and
=clal_dwg.dll=.  The release format deliberately separates =binaries= and
=libraries= archives, while the normal installer downloads and overlays both
into one prefix.  A complete installation must therefore be usable without a
development checkout, compiler, manually-set library path, or an explicit
ASDF command from the user.

* Cause

There are two halves to native codec availability, and the distributed
standalone currently only satisfies the filesystem half.

1. =clautolisp/drawing-dwg= contains the CFFI declarations and registers the
   =:DWG= reader/writer when =drawing-dwg/source/codec.lisp= is loaded.
2. =clautolisp/clautolisp-tool= does not depend on
   =clautolisp/drawing-dwg=.  The saved executable therefore contains the
   drawing core but not necessarily the code which registers the DWG codec.
3. =drawing-dwg/source/bindings.lisp= already has partial path resolution:
   =$CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR=, a development-tree candidate, and an installed
   =PREFIX/lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= candidate derived from the installed
   ASDF source directory.  That resolver cannot help if the system containing
   it is never loaded.
4. On Windows, once the system is loaded, the loader must first open the
   adjacent =libredwg.dll= by absolute pathname and then =clal_dwg.dll=.  On
   ELF/Mach-O the shim's =$ORIGIN=/=@loader_path= rpath is intended to resolve
   the adjacent dependency.

The result is a misleading split state: the packaging and build logs prove
that the codec was distributed, but =clautolisp.drawing= has no registered DWG
writer because the activation code never ran.

* Required behaviour

At startup, or lazily on the first operation requiring the DWG codec, the
standalone clautolisp runtime must:

1. determine the installation prefix from the running installed program and
   its dispatch layout, without assuming the build directory or current
   working directory;
2. select =lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= for the actual runtime platform;
3. load the platform dependency and shim with
   =cffi:load-foreign-library= in the required order;
4. load/retain the Lisp side of =clautolisp/drawing-dwg= and register the
   =:DWG= reader and writer;
5. retain =$CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR= as an explicit override, not as a mandatory
   installation step.

The installed prefix is relocatable.  Moving or unpacking it under a different
absolute directory, including a directory containing spaces, must not break
native-library discovery.  The runtime must not embed the staging path from
the machine which produced the release.

If the user installed only the =binaries= archive, or if a native object is
present but unloadable, the eventual DWG operation must report a diagnostic
which distinguishes at least:

- codec Lisp module unavailable/not activated;
- shim absent, with all attempted pathnames;
- dependency absent;
- dynamic-loader rejection, including the underlying CFFI/OS error.

It must not collapse all of these cases into =no writer codec registered=.

* Fix direction

Bake =clautolisp/drawing-dwg= into the standalone program, or provide an
equivalent explicit optional-module activation phase in the program startup.
The native library itself may remain lazily loaded: =ensure-shim-loaded=
already defers =cffi:load-foreign-library= until the first DWG conversion.

Centralise installation-prefix discovery so other distributed native
extensions can reuse it.  The resolver should start from the installed
launcher/executable layout and may use the installed ASDF source directory as
a corroborating candidate, but must not require ASDF sources merely to locate
a native library shipped beside the program.

Do not solve this by adding a CI-only =LD_LIBRARY_PATH=, modifying the global
Windows =PATH=, or hard-coding =/opt/local=.  Those hide the packaging/runtime
contract instead of making an installed release self-contained.

* Reproduction

From a release or the output of =make install-programs install-libraries=:

1. unpack/install the program and libraries into a fresh arbitrary prefix;
2. unset =CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR= and any project-specific library-path
   variables;
3. run the installed =bin/clautolisp= from an unrelated working directory;
4. create a drawing and save it as DWG through the Cador/ActiveX
   =Document.SaveAs= path, or call the drawing persistence API;
5. observe that the native files are present under
   =lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= but the operation reports no registered
   =:DWG= writer.

The SCHME+ reproducer is:

#+begin_example
make test-cad-activex-alfe CAD=clautolisp
#+end_example

* Acceptance criteria

- The standalone program built for a release includes or automatically
  activates =clautolisp/drawing-dwg=; the =:DWG= codec is registered without
  an explicit user-side =(asdf:load-system ...)=.
- A complete installed release finds its adjacent platform-native libraries
  with =CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR= unset and successfully writes and reads a DWG.
- The check runs from an unrelated current directory after relocating the
  installation prefix to a path containing spaces.
- Automated packaged-runtime tests cover Linux =.so= and Windows =.dll=
  layouts.  The Windows test proves that =libredwg.dll= is loaded before the
  shim; the Linux test proves that the adjacent dependency is resolved.
- Installing only the binaries archive fails a DWG operation with an explicit
  missing-native-library diagnostic, not =no writer codec registered=.
- =$CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR= continues to override automatic discovery.
- The SCHME+ =creation et fermeture d'un DWG temporaire= scenario no longer
  fails for lack of a registered writer.

* Related

- =issues/closed/drawing-codec-libredwg-submodule.issue=
- =issues/closed/release-artefacts.issue=
- =issues/closed/install-sources-with-libraries.issue=
- =issues/closed/cador-schme-a1-activex-coverage.issue=
