- =issues/open/dwg-write-rejects-a-drawing-built-in-memory.issue= — what
  became visible once the codec was actually in the program.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.99: the codec is in the program, and the
  libraries are found from the program

Both halves named in /Cause/ are addressed, and the result is verified
against the BUILT executable rather than a test image.

1. *The codec is baked in.* =clautolisp/clautolisp-tool= now depends on
   =clautolisp/drawing-dwg=, with the reason spelled out beside the
   identical case for the compiler: nothing below the program depends on
   it, so the program that assembles the image has to. The NATIVE library
   stays lazily loaded (=ensure-shim-loaded= still defers
   =cffi:load-foreign-library= to the first conversion), so this costs
   nothing at startup and a binaries-only installation still runs.

2. *The libraries are found from the RUNNING PROGRAM.* New in
   drawing-dwg/source/bindings.lisp:
   - =running-program-pathname= — the one implementation-specific spot
     (=sb-ext:*runtime-pathname*= / =ccl::kernel-path=);
   - =running-program-prefix-candidates= — the prefix for the two shipped
     layouts, =<PREFIX>/bin/…= and anywhere below =<PREFIX>/libexec/…=,
     deepest =libexec= winning, case-insensitively so LIBEXEC and libexec
     are one directory on MS-Windows;
   - =native-library-directories= — the whole search order:
     =CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR=, the development tree, the program's
     prefixes with =lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= appended, and last the
     installed-ASDF-source prefix that used to be the only installed
     candidate. Nothing embeds a staging path, and duplicates are
     removed.

3. *The diagnostics distinguish the cases.* A missing shim lists every
   pathname tried and names the platform directory a release must carry;
   on MS-Windows a shim present WITHOUT libredwg.dll beside it says so
   explicitly instead of letting the loader fail obscurely; and a loader
   rejection is reported with the file and the loader's own words. The
   one case that can no longer happen is the one that was reported:
   "codec Lisp module not activated".

Verified end to end on the built binary: =(vla-saveas d "…dwg")= used to
answer /no writer codec registered for format :DWG/ and now reaches
libredwg. And =native-library-directories= in a process started as
=/usr/bin/sbcl= lists =/usr/lib/clautolisp/linux/x86-64/=, i.e. the
prefix really is derived from the program.

Tests (clautolisp/drawing-dwg/tests/dwg-tests.lisp, suite 20/20; full
clautolisp suite 9993 checks 0 failures):
=dwg-tool-program-depends-on-the-codec= (the dependency cannot be
dropped again), =dwg-running-program-prefix-candidates-knows-both-layouts=
(both layouts, a prefix containing its own libexec, and no wrong prefix
claimed for neither), =dwg-native-library-directories-order-and-override=
(order, the override first, the program-derived candidate present, no
duplicates).

** What is NOT done, and why

- *A complete release does not yet write a DWG*, so the second
  acceptance criterion is not met -- but not for a packaging reason. With
  the codec in place, writing a drawing BUILT in the session fails inside
  libredwg with error 2048, while a drawing READ from a DWG round-trips
  (302706 bytes written). Filed as
  =dwg-write-rejects-a-drawing-built-in-memory= with the diff-the-two-DXFs
  recipe. The SCHME+ scenario needs that one.
- *No packaged-runtime test yet.* The criteria ask for automated tests
  over a real installed prefix for Linux =.so= and Windows =.dll=,
  including relocation to a path with spaces. The pieces exist (the
  discovery is now a pure function of the program's pathname, and
  alfe's verify:installed-prefix:windows job is the model for the Windows
  half), but a job that runs =make install-programs install-libraries=
  into a temp prefix and drives the installed binary is still to be
  written.
- *Centralising the prefix discovery* (so a second native extension can
  reuse it) is deliberately deferred: the helpers are exported and
  documented as the reuse point, but moving them into
  =clautolisp/configuration= before a second caller exists would add a
  cross-system API on speculation. Recorded here so the next extension
  does the move rather than a copy.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.105: the packaged check exists and passes on Linux

=scripts/verify-packaged-dwg.sh= is the outside-in check the criteria ask
for, and it runs green here. It does what a user does, and nothing else:

1. =make install-programs install-libraries= into a prefix that does not
   exist yet and whose path CONTAINS A SPACE;
2. runs the installed binary from =/=, with =CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR=,
   =LD_LIBRARY_PATH= and =DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH= UNSET;
3. writes a DWG through the ActiveX =SaveAs= path and checks the bytes;
4. MOVES the prefix and repeats, since a release is staged, archived and
   unpacked somewhere else entirely -- nothing may embed its build path;
5. installs the PROGRAMS ONLY and requires the missing-library
   diagnostic, not "no writer codec registered".

Measured on 2026-09-26:

#+begin_example
packaged-dwg: program: …/a prefix with spaces/bin/clautolisp
packaged-dwg: native:  …/a prefix with spaces/lib/clautolisp/linux/x86-64/clal_dwg.so
packaged-dwg: running from / with no overrides
packaged-dwg:   WROTE-DWG
packaged-dwg: wrote 302711 bytes
packaged-dwg: moving the prefix, to prove nothing embedded its path
packaged-dwg:   WROTE-DWG
packaged-dwg: installing the programs WITHOUT the libraries
packaged-dwg:   …: the DWG native library clal_dwg.so was not found. Looked in:
packaged-dwg:   …/drawing-dwg/source/clal_dwg.so, …/binaries only/lib/clautolisp/
packaged-dwg:   linux/x86-64/clal_dwg.so. A release must ship it under …
packaged-dwg: OK: a complete installed release writes a DWG, before and after being moved
#+end_example

Step 5 needs the DEVELOPMENT TREE's copy hidden to mean anything -- the
installed program still probes it, because the dev-tree candidate resolves
through the ASDF system it was built from, a path that does not exist on a
machine which only unpacked a release. The script moves it aside and
restores it, with a trap for an interrupted run.

CI: =verify:packaged-dwg:linux= (poseidon, manual, 45 min) builds the
vendored libredwg and the program images and runs the script. Manual
because it is minutes of C and Lisp compilation and nothing depends on
its result.

** The Windows =.dll= half — the job exists (clautolisp 2.2.111)

Not rewritten in PowerShell after all. The runner drives PowerShell, but
=scripts/avec-bash.ps1= already normalises the PowerShell → MSYS2 bash handoff
for the release lane (MSYS2's bash over Git's, =MSYS2_PATH_TYPE=inherit=,
MINGW64 before UCRT64, CC set on the bash side), so the platform-neutral
checker is REUSED instead of duplicated. Duplicating 140 lines of shell in
another language would have made two things to keep in step, and the first
divergence would have been silent.

What landed:

- =scripts/verify-packaged-dwg.sh= is now platform-aware: it detects the host
  (=uname=), looks the program up under =.exe= as well (=test -x= does not
  append it on MSYS2), and gained the Windows-only fourth case — install the
  libraries, REMOVE =libredwg.dll=, and insist the program answers "installed
  but its dependency libredwg.dll is not beside it" rather than a codec
  complaint. A save that SUCCEEDS there is a failure of the check, not a pass:
  it means the loader found a =libredwg.dll= elsewhere on =PATH=, so the run
  proves nothing about a user's machine, and the script says exactly that.
  The libraries phase shipping =clal_dwg.dll= without =libredwg.dll= is itself
  reported as a packaging failure.
- =scripts/verify-packaged-dwg-windows.sh= — the bash side: sources
  =environnement-windows.sh=, =make build-libraries=, =make build-programs=,
  then the checker. A file, not inline YAML, for the reason
  =scripts/release-windows.sh= records: PowerShell expands =$(...)= in a
  double-quoted string itself, so an inline =$(cygpath -u …)= would be
  evaluated by PowerShell, which has no =cygpath=.
- =verify:packaged-dwg:windows= (.gitlab/native.yml, manual, 60 min,
  =.gate-windows-soft=). Deliberately NOT =.gate-windows-cad-*=: this needs
  MS-Windows, not AutoCAD, so it must neither queue behind the CAD sweep nor
  be failed by it. It checks =$LASTEXITCODE= after the handoff — PowerShell
  does not fail a step on a native program's non-zero exit, the trap the
  release lane documents.

=make check-release= (all five packaging checks) passes with the job added.

*** It ran — and found a real defect (2026-09-26, job 16756197963)

The lane ran on its first opportunity (the Windows runner came online and
=.gate-windows-soft= schedules it automatically) and FAILED, which is the lane
earning its keep. Everything up to the check worked: libredwg built from the
submodule (8.5 min), the images built, and the install produced the right
layout —

#+begin_example
packaged-dwg: program: .../a prefix with spaces/bin/clautolisp.exe
packaged-dwg: native:  .../a prefix with spaces/lib/clautolisp/windows/x86-64/clal_dwg.dll
#+end_example

— and then the save failed:

#+begin_example
COM-SAVE-FAILED: the DWG native library
  C:/gitlab-runner/builds/.../clautolisp/drawing-dwg/source/clal_dwg.dll
  is installed but its dependency libredwg.dll is not beside it in
  C:/gitlab-runner/builds/.../clautolisp/drawing-dwg/source/.
#+end_example

*The installed program loaded the DEVELOPMENT TREE's shim, not its own.*
=native-library-directories= lists the dev-tree ASDF path before the program's
=lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= — correct for a developer — and
=ensure-shim-loaded= took the first candidate that EXISTED, then died on it.
The dev copy on that machine has no =libredwg.dll= beside it (cmake puts it
elsewhere), so nothing could be saved although the installed pair was complete.

*And the same wrong pick was invisible on Linux*, because the dev copy's rpath
resolves its dependency: **the Linux lane had been passing its first two cases
without ever loading the installed library.** The check was not checking what
it claimed, on any machine that has a build tree — which is every CI machine.

*** Fixed — clautolisp 2.2.118, in two parts

*The ORDER is the root cause* (2.2.118). =%NATIVE-LIBRARY-DIRECTORIES= is now a
pure function over the four pieces, and it puts the RUNNING PROGRAM's own
=lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/= BEFORE the development tree:

: 1. $CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR   2. the program's own lib/  3. the dev tree  4. the installed ASDF prefix

A library shipped beside the program belongs to that program; a build tree
merely happens to exist on the same machine. A developer loses nothing, and
that is what makes the change safe: running from a checkout the program is the
host Lisp (or a dev image under =tools/clautolisp/bin/=), neither of which owns
a =lib/clautolisp/…=, so the dev tree is still what gets found. The order
settles only the case where BOTH exist — an installed program on a machine with
a checkout, i.e. every CI machine.

Measured on the BUILT binary, reproducing the Windows situation on Linux (the
installed prefix complete, the dev copy present but unloadable — a zero-length
=clal_dwg.so=):

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp -norc -q -l probe.lsp        # installed program, broken dev copy present
WROTE-DWG                                  # 302711 bytes, and NO "skipped" line:
                                           # its own library was taken first
$ CLAUTOLISP_DWG_LIBDIR=<broken dir> clautolisp -norc -q -l probe.lsp
clautolisp: skipped the DWG native library .../clal_dwg.so: the dynamic loader
  refused it: ... file too short.
WROTE-DWG                                  # the explicit override still ranks
                                           # first, is skipped, and the search goes on
#+end_example

The second line is the fall-through below doing its job, and it is also the
proof that the diagnostic fires at all.

*The FALL-THROUGH keeps a broken candidate from ending the search* (2.2.117):

- =%TRY-LOAD-SHIM= attempts one candidate (its MS-Windows dependency first) and
  returns a REASON instead of signalling. =%SELECT-USABLE-SHIM= walks the
  candidates and takes the first that actually LOADS; every skipped one is
  reported on =*error-output*= (=skipped the DWG native library …: …=), so a
  developer with a broken local build cannot silently end up running an
  installed, older library. When none loads, the error names each one and the
  loader's words for it. The candidate ORDER is unchanged: a developer's own
  build still wins when it works.
- =scripts/verify-packaged-dwg.sh= now hides the development tree's shim for
  the WHOLE check rather than only for its last case. That is what makes cases
  1 and 2 mean anything on a machine with a checkout: the question is what a
  machine that only unpacked a release does, and such a machine has no dev tree.

Measured on Linux after the fix — the first run in which the check really
loaded the installed library:

#+begin_example
packaged-dwg: hid the development tree's clal_dwg.so for the whole check
packaged-dwg: native:  /tmp/.../a prefix with spaces/lib/clautolisp/linux/x86-64/clal_dwg.so
packaged-dwg:   WROTE-DWG        (302711 bytes)
packaged-dwg:   WROTE-DWG        (after moving the prefix)
packaged-dwg: the missing library is named, with the paths tried
packaged-dwg: OK: a complete installed release writes a DWG, before and after being moved
#+end_example

Tests: eight in =drawing-dwg/tests/dwg-tests.lisp= — four over the ORDER
(=%NATIVE-LIBRARY-DIRECTORIES= as a pure function: the whole order, the
program before the dev tree, a developer left with the dev tree, several
program prefixes kept in order; plus one over the real function asserting it is
duplicate-free and never puts the dev tree first) and four over
=%SELECT-USABLE-SHIM=
with injected probe/try functions — so the Windows case is reproducible on
Linux, with no library on disk: fall through an existing-but-unusable
candidate (the reported case, asserting the skip is reported), prefer the first
USABLE one (the dev build still wins when it works), ignore a candidate that
does not exist, and report every rejection when none works. drawing-dwg suite
38 checks, 0 failures.

